.TH BTFS 4
.SH NAME
btfs, btbench \- Bluetooth Low Energy as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt/btfs
[
.B -u
] [
.BI -m " mountpoint"
] [
.BI -k " keyfile"
] [
.BI -a " seconds"
] [
.I controller ...
]
.PP
.B bt/btbench
[
.I controller
[
.I mountpoint
[
.I keyfile
[
.I mode
[
.I controller2
]
]
]
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Btfs
is a Bluetooth Low Energy stack.  All of it above the controller is
an ordinary user program: the kernel carries HCI packets and does
nothing else, which it already knows how to do \- a byte stream
through
.IR devuart (3)
for a UART-attached controller, endpoints through
.IR devusb (3)
for a USB one.
.PP
Bluetooth's own structure is a hierarchy \- a controller, the devices
it can hear, the services each device has, the characteristics inside
a service \- so
.I btfs
serves it as one, and that name space is the whole interface.  There
are no other calls: a program uses Bluetooth here by opening, reading
and writing files.
.PP
.I Controller
names the transport.  For the default H4 transport it is a byte
stream and, optionally, its speed and whether to use RTS/CTS flow
control:
.IP
.EX
bt/btfs /dev/eia1!115200!fc
.EE
.PP
It may instead be a
.IR dial (2)
string, which is how a machine whose only serial port is its console
reaches a controller elsewhere; the framing is the same either way.
.PP
Several may be named, and each becomes one numbered directory:
.IP
.EX
bt/btfs /dev/eia1!115200!fc /dev/eia2!115200!fc
.EE
.PP
gives
.B /net/bt/0
and
.BR /net/bt/1 .
They are two radios in one program and not two programs, so the node
table, the mount point and the key file are shared while the devices,
the links, the scanning and the bonds are not.
With
.B -u
the transport is USB instead, and
.I controller
is the device directory under
.B /dev/usb
.RB ( ep3.0 ,
say), or empty to take the first Bluetooth controller found there.
.PP
.B -m
gives the mount point, default
.BR /net/bt .
.B -k
names a file the bonding keys are read from at start and written back
to as they are made; without it they last only as long as
.I btfs
does.
.SS The name space
.TF /net/bt/0/remote/ADDR/gatt
.PD
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/addr
The controller's own address.  There is one numbered directory per
controller, in the order the devices were given on the command line,
and nothing is shared between them: a device heard on one radio, a
link, a scan and a bond all belong to the radio they happened on.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/version
What the controller says it is.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/state
One line: the address, whether it is scanning, whether it is
advertising, how many devices have been heard, how many connections
are open, and the controller's ACL packet size and buffer count.  A
.B scodropped
count is added when the controller has sent any SCO packets: there is
no audio here, nothing above the transport has a use for them, and
they are counted rather than dropped in silence, where they could not
be told from a transport losing them.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/scan
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/bonds
One line per bond: the peer, its address type, the keys, and
.BI on " addr" ,
the controller the bond was made on.  A long term key is agreed
between one peripheral and one radio, and the peripheral stores this
side's identity address alongside it, so a bond made on one radio is
not a bond on another and is not offered to it.  The key file named by
.B -k
holds every controller's bonds together and each line says whose it
is; a line that does not say, from a file written when there was only
one controller to make a bond on, is offered to every controller and
btfs says so once.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/ctl
Writing
.B "scan on"
or
.B "scan off"
starts and stops scanning.
.B "connect \fIaddr\fP [public|random]",
.BI "disconnect " addr
and
.BI "pair " addr
act on one device.
.BI "params " "min max latency timeout"
on a device's own
.B ctl
asks the controller to run the link at those parameters: the
connection interval in units of 1.25ms, how many connection events the
peripheral may skip, and the supervision timeout in units of 10ms.
Values a controller would refuse are refused here instead of being
sent.  A peripheral cannot change them itself and asks over L2CAP; a
request that is accepted is carried out, which is why one that cannot
be is refused rather than accepted and forgotten.
.B "advertise on"
and
.B "advertise off"
start and stop advertising, and
.BI "advname " name
sets the name the advertisement carries; it can be changed while
advertising is running and the peer sees the new one on the next
advertisement.  Advertising is connectable: a peer that finds this
side can open a link to it, and that link appears under
.B remote
like any other, with
.B state
saying
.B peripheral
because the peer opened it rather than this side.  Nothing above
L2CAP answers on such a link yet \- there is no GATT server here, so
a peer that goes on to ask for attributes gets no reply.
.BI "l2cap " psm
on a device's own
.B ctl
opens an LE credit-based channel to that PSM, written as four
hexadecimal digits, and
.BI "unl2cap " psm
closes it.  A channel that the peer refuses fails with the peer's own
reason \(em
.B "PSM not supported"
and the rest \(em rather than with a word of this program's choosing.
.B connect
waits for the controller to establish the link and fails with
.B "connection timed out"
followed by what the controller said, if it said anything; a
connection that completes just as the wait runs out is returned rather
than thrown away, and one that completes after that is closed when it
is found.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/
A device that has been heard from, named by its address written most
significant byte first \- or, once a bond gives it one, by its
identity address, so a device that rotates a resolvable private
address keeps one directory rather than gaining one every few minutes.
It holds
.BR addr ,
which is the address it is answering on now and changes under a
rotating device,
.B identity
(the identity address behind it and the address it was resolved from,
or
.B none
for a device that has not distributed an identity key),
.B type
(public or random),
.B rssi
and
.B name
from its advertisement,
.B adv
(the whole advertisement in hexadecimal),
.BR state ,
and a
.B ctl
that takes
.BR connect ,
.B disconnect
and
.B pair
without an address.
.B state
says how long ago the device was last heard from when it is not
connected, and what the link is running at when it is, and which side
opened it \-
.B central
for a link this side opened,
.B peripheral
for one a peer opened to it.
.PP
A device that stops answering is forgotten after the time given by
.B -a
(five minutes by default; 0 keeps everything), so a scan in a busy
room does not fill this directory for ever.  Three things are never
forgotten: a connected device, because it is plainly there and an
advertisement is not how it is being talked to; a device with an
identity from a bond, because a bond exists in order to find that
device again; and every device while scanning is off, because then
nothing is arriving and age says nothing about what is there.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/gatt/
Appears when the device is connected: a directory per service, named
by its UUID, holding a file per characteristic, named by its UUID.
If the device has a Service Changed characteristic it is subscribed
to, and an indication on it rebuilds this whole directory on the same
link \- so a device that rearranges its database is followed rather
than believed on the copy walked first.  Files open across that are
gone, including
.B .notify
streams, whose subscriptions stood against handles that may no longer
mean the same thing; a client that wants one reopens it.  The
subscription to Service Changed itself is not rewritten unless its own
descriptor moved, since it belongs to the link rather than to a walk
of the database.
A service that includes others has an
.B included
file listing them, one per line as UUID, first handle and last, and
each is a directory here too \- including a secondary service, which
primary discovery does not report and which would otherwise be
unreachable.
.PP
A value longer than the link's ATT MTU is read in pieces with Read
Blob and written in pieces with Prepare Write, committed with one
Execute Write, so neither direction is limited by the MTU.
Reading one reads the characteristic off the device; writing one
writes it.
.PD
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/l2cap/
One file per open LE credit-based channel, named by its PSM in four
hexadecimal digits.  This is not part of the GATT tree and does not go
away when the device says its database has moved: a channel is not an
attribute and a Service Changed indication says nothing about it.  It
does go away with the link, along with everything else the link was
carrying.
.PP
One write is one SDU and one read is the next SDU whole.  A write may
wait: credits are how the peer says it is ready, and a peer that has
granted none has said it is not, so the bytes wait rather than being
thrown away.  A read that offers a smaller buffer than the SDU fails
saying how big the SDU was, because there is nowhere to put the
remainder and a short read would lose it silently; the most this side
will accept is 512 bytes, so a reader that offers that much never sees
it.  The device's
.B state
has a line per open channel giving the PSM, both channel identifiers,
and the SDU and PDU sizes the two sides settled on.
.PP
The flow control is real in both directions.  Going out, an SDU is cut
into PDUs at the peer's maximum and a credit is spent on each.  Coming
in, credits are granted for as many PDUs as there is room for and
given back when a reader takes the SDU they carried, so nothing is
ever dropped for want of somewhere to put it \(em unlike the fixed
channels above, where a consumer that falls behind loses frames
because the alternative is stopping the controller.  A peer that sends
a segment it has no credit for, or announces an SDU longer than the
MTU, has broken the only rule that bounds this side's memory, and the
channel is closed with that reason rather than the segment absorbed.
.PP
So an echo service on PSM 0x0080 is
.IP
.EX
echo l2cap 0080 > /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/ctl
echo hello > /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/l2cap/0080
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/l2cap/0080
.EE
.PP
A characteristic that notifies or indicates also has a
.IB uuid .notify
beside it.  Opening that file subscribes to the characteristic, each
read returns the next notification whole, and closing it
unsubscribes.
.PP
So a battery level is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19
.EE
.PP
and watching it change is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19.notify
.EE
.SS Btbench
.I Btbench
starts
.I btfs
on the transport it is given and then drives the whole stack through
its files, printing what happens at each step: the controller, a
scan, what answered, a connection, the remote device's whole GATT
database, a characteristic read, three notifications, pairing, and a
disconnection.  It is the test program, and it runs unchanged under
.IR emu (1)
and inside the native kernels.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bt
.br
.B /appl/lib/bt
.br
.B /module/bt.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bt (2),
.IR dial (2),
.IR devuart (3),
.IR devusb (3),
.IR usbd (8)
.SH BUGS
.PP
This is Bluetooth Low Energy only.  There is no Bluetooth Classic:
no inquiry, no SDP, no RFCOMM, no HID, no PAN, and no audio.  L2CAP
here is the fixed channels an LE link uses \- ATT, LE signalling and
security management \- and the length and CID framing under them;
there are no connection-oriented channels.
.PP
Pairing is LE legacy pairing with a zero temporary key, which is Just
Works: it establishes an encrypted link and a bond, and it gives no
protection at all against a man in the middle.  There is no passkey
entry and no numeric comparison.  LE Secure Connections is not
implemented \- it needs P-256 elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman, which is
not here \- so a device that insists on it will not pair, and says so.
.PP
The USB transport
.RB ( -u )
is written and compiles but has never moved a packet.  There is no
USB Bluetooth controller on the bench and no emulated one, so nothing
about it is demonstrated; only the H4 transport is.
.PP
On
.B pc64
the H4 transport over the second serial port makes the kernel fault:
sustained traffic on that uart ends in a general protection violation
at the
.B IRETQ
in
.B _intrr
.RB ( os/inferno/pc64/l.s ),
with a corrupt interrupt frame.  That is a fault in the kernel's own
interrupt return, not in this stack: the same Dis code over a
.IR dial (2)
transport completes on the same
.B pc64
kernel and on
.BR riscv64 .
See
.B doc/inferno-ports.ms.
.PP
Only the initiating (central) side is implemented.  This stack does
not advertise and cannot be connected to.
.PP
A resolvable private address is resolved against every stored bond's
identity resolving key, so a device that rotates its address \- which
most modern peripherals do \- stays one device under one directory,
named by its identity, and the bond made with it remains reachable.
Resolution is done here rather than by handing the keys to the
controller's resolving list.
.PP
The name a directory is given when the device is first heard from is
kept.  So a device first seen from a private address, and paired only
afterwards, keeps the directory named by that first private address
even though its later addresses resolve to it: the name is settled
before there is a key to resolve it with, and a directory a client may
be holding open is not renamed underneath it.  Its
.B identity
file says what it really is.
.PP
A bond read from the
.B -k
key file at startup resolves addresses just as one made in the session
does, so a device bonded with earlier is recognised again without
pairing.
.PP
Pairing is still LE legacy Just Works, so the identity key is
distributed without protection against a man in the middle, and
resolution is only as trustworthy as the pairing that produced the
key.
.PP
.B scripts/btbench.sh
runs
.I btbench
under
.I emu
against the controller model in
.B scripts/bthci.py ,
in two phases -- pairing, then meeting the bond again from a key file
\- and exits non-zero if either fails.
.PP
.B doc/bluetooth-plan.ms
lists what is missing in the order the pieces block each other, and
plans the 9front implementation.
